---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import riceShot from "../assets/images/hyprland-rice.png";

const title = `${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`;
// The home page renders its own terminal hero rather than the theme's image
// Hero, so it drives BaseLayout directly. Home lives at "/", where the theme's
// card-title transition name collapses to this constant; matching it keeps the
// shared-element transition running when you arrive from the nav wordmark.
const transitionName = "card-title-";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
  mainAttrs={{ class: "page-home" }}
>
  <section class="term-hero" slot="hero" aria-label={title}>
    <div class="term-window">
      <div class="term-bar" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="term-dots"></span>
        <span class="term-bar-name">~/{courseMeta.code.toLowerCase()}</span>
      </div>
      <div class="term-hero-body">
        <p class="term-cmd">
          <span class="term-prompt">❯</span> ./ricing.sh<span class="term-cursor" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="term-hero-title" transition:name={transitionName}>{title}</h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="lead">{courseMeta.description}</p>

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will build one desktop. Over the semester you will choose a compositor
    and a window manager, write and version the dotfiles that describe them,
    settle a palette and a typeface, compose a status bar, and photograph the
    result. Then you will do the harder thing: make it reproducible, so that a
    stranger with a bare machine can arrive at the same desktop you did, and
    write an honest account of the hours it cost against the benefit it
    returned.
  </p>

  <figure class="rice-shot">
    <Image
      src={riceShot}
      alt="A Hyprland desktop: Neovim editing a hyprpaper wallpaper script on the left, a btop system monitor and an audio visualiser on the right, over a monochrome wallpaper of a top-hatted figure."
      widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1920]}
      sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 60rem, 100vw"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>One desktop, end to end: the dotfiles, the status readout, and the wallpaper, all of a piece.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Students who already live in a terminal and want to think about why, and
    students who have admired a screenshot and wondered what it would take to
    earn one. You should be comfortable on the Linux command line and with git.
    You do not need a design background; you do need a willingness to defend
    your choices out loud and in writing.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve-week arc, from the origins of the term to the closing gallery.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly lab: what to bring, what happens, and what leaves the room.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .rice-shot {
    margin: 0 0 2.5rem;
  }

  .rice-shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
  }

  .rice-shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
